# Proof of Work (HARD RULE)

Agents are superb at satisfying explicit criteria and unreliable at reading
minds, and they overclaim completion. Both facts are designed around here.

## Spec before code
A task is not `ready` until acceptance criteria exist (01). Prefer executable
tests as the criteria; write or approve them BEFORE implementation. UI work
gets a concrete checklist plus before/after screenshots. If criteria are vague,
the correct move is to fix the spec, not to guess harder. Spec quality is the
throughput ceiling at scale; treat a bad spec as a P1 defect.

## Gate ladder (every land climbs it; depth scales with risk)
- L0 machine, per worktree, always: compile, typecheck, lint, tests (full
  suite or the documented scoped set), secret scan, diff coverage on changed
  lines. Red at L0 = the task goes back to `in-progress`; nothing above runs.
- L1 independent review, always: a reviewer agent that did NOT write the
  change, briefed with ONLY the spec and the diff (not the writer's chat).
  Where available, use a different model or a fresh adversarial persona than
  the writer, so reviewer errors do not correlate with writer errors.
- L2 adversarial, for risk high: auth, payments, money math, migrations,
  native, and anything user-facing that can break. A red-team pass actively
  tries to break the change: edge cases, races, injection, rounding, replay.
- L3 human, only for risk irreversible: production deploy, store submission,
  destructive data operations. Present the diff, the evidence, and a rollback
  plan, then wait.

Writer != reviewer != supervisor, always. The agent that wrote a change never
approves it; the agent that planned the split never audits its own plan.

## Merge queue (serialize the land, not the work)
Writers commit on feature branches in their own worktrees and do NOT push.
Finished slices enter a queue. The supervisor lands them one at a time, runs
the FULL L0 gate after EACH land (never one batch gate at the end), halts the
queue on first red, and pushes only after all queued lands are green. On red:
one restorer returns the trunk to last-known-good, then the gate that let the
failure through is fixed BEFORE the queue resumes. Never leave the operator on
a broken build; never merge then revert.

## Evidence or it did not happen
Every land attaches artifacts, linked from the task ledger entry and the
HANDOFF entry: test output, build log, screenshot for UI, curl or client
transcript for APIs, device/simulator run note for mobile. A completion claim
without evidence is treated as NOT done, and saying "done" without evidence is
itself a rule violation to record in LEARNINGS.

## Verify before handoff to the operator
Do NOT ask the operator to open a URL, refresh a page, or confirm a visual fix
until the agent has:
1. Confirmed the server/process is running on the stated host:port (via its
   lease, then the process).
2. Loaded the URL via the Cursor browser tool or equivalent. Mandatory. If no
   such tool is available, say so explicitly and state what was verified
   instead; never silently skip.
3. Fixed connection-refused, blank page, 404, and obvious runtime errors found
   in that check.
Use runtime-injected test credentials for sign-in flows when the repo documents
them (05). Report PASS/FAIL with the evidence. Never outsource first-pass QA
the agent can run itself.

## Rebuild discipline
No full rebuild until L0 is green OR a known-good backup exists. One rebuild
owner per worktree. A failed rebuild restores the backup; never leave a
half-written output dir.
